{"uid":"64fd0ff7419e1315","name":"test_review_ladder_exhaustion_with_budget_remaining_holds_for_the_operator","fullName":"tests.flows.test_flow_review_replan_pause#test_review_ladder_exhaustion_with_budget_remaining_holds_for_the_operator","historyId":"0b950edea94ce51ef900222867d4ac74","time":{"start":1791931191332,"stop":1791931193965,"duration":2633},"description":"GH #209 defect 2, at the flow tier. This node USED to pin the\n    opposite outcome, deliberately: an unparseable review reply exhausted\n    the ladder and the engine then applied \"today's ordinary archive-with-\n    safe-default consequences\" -- approach 1 ARCHIVED, a composite PRD\n    written, `verdict:review:rejected@1` recorded, the run replanned onto\n    approach 2 and succeeded there (`assert st[\"approach\"] == 2`). That is\n    the defect: a reviewer that never produced a parseable verdict at all\n    was scored exactly like a reviewer that read the work and refused it,\n    and it cost an approach. It now HOLDS instead -- `pauseReason:\n    \"review_contract_unrepaired\"`, approach 1 unarchived, no composite PRD,\n    `verdict:review:unreviewed@1`, one `operator_escalation` -- and a\n    `POST /resume` re-enters review over the SAME approach, where the\n    stub's next (well-formed) reply verifies.\n\n    Every assertion the old outcome did not itself dictate survives\n    verbatim: `review-count == \"2\"` (one exhausted real invocation, one\n    passing one -- the ladder's uncharged re-asks never advance the\n    marker), every task `completed`, `usage.byPhase.contract` non-empty,\n    and budget genuinely left over. The two that inverted are named above.\n    Sibling of `test_the_last_approach_pauses_for_the_operator_and_a_\n    resume_verifies`, whose shape this now follows exactly.","descriptionHtml":"<p>GH #209 defect 2, at the flow tier. This node USED to pin the<br />\nopposite outcome, deliberately: an unparseable review reply exhausted<br />\nthe ladder and the engine then applied &quot;today's ordinary archive-with-<br />\nsafe-default consequences&quot; -- approach 1 ARCHIVED, a composite PRD<br />\nwritten, <code>verdict:review:rejected@1</code> recorded, the run replanned onto<br />\napproach 2 and succeeded there (<code>assert st[&quot;approach&quot;] == 2</code>). That is<br />\nthe defect: a reviewer that never produced a parseable verdict at all<br />\nwas scored exactly like a reviewer that read the work and refused it,<br />\nand it cost an approach. It now HOLDS instead -- <code>pauseReason: &quot;review_contract_unrepaired&quot;</code>, approach 1 unarchived, no composite PRD,<br />\n<code>verdict:review:unreviewed@1</code>, one <code>operator_escalation</code> -- and a<br />\n<code>POST /resume</code> re-enters review over the SAME approach, where the<br />\nstub's next (well-formed) reply verifies.</p>\n<pre><code>Every assertion the old outcome did not itself dictate survives\nverbatim: `review-count == &quot;2&quot;` (one exhausted real invocation, one\npassing one -- the ladder's uncharged re-asks never advance the\nmarker), every task `completed`, `usage.byPhase.contract` non-empty,\nand budget genuinely left over. The two that inverted are named above.\nSibling of `test_the_last_approach_pauses_for_the_operator_and_a_\nresume_verifies`, whose shape this now follows exactly.\n</code></pre>\n","status":"passed","flaky":false,"newFailed":false,"newBroken":false,"newPassed":false,"retriesCount":0,"retriesStatusChange":false,"beforeStages":[{"name":"monkeypatch","time":{"start":1791931191278,"stop":1791931191278,"duration":0},"status":"passed","steps":[],"attachments":[],"parameters":[],"shouldDisplayMessage":false,"stepsCount":0,"attachmentsCount":0,"hasContent":false,"attachmentStep":false},{"name":"_isolate_ambient_home_in_process","time":{"start":1791931191279,"stop":1791931191295,"duration":16},"status":"passed","steps":[],"attachments":[],"parameters":[],"shouldDisplayMessage":false,"stepsCount":0,"attachmentsCount":0,"ha